Chapter: StringBuilderAlias
  cites Foreword chapter StringBuilder

 StringBuilder is a mutable record and sb-append writes its parts in place, so
 handing one builder to two owners would let each believe it holds the builder
 alone. Here the builder arrives as a declared parameter and is appended to
 twice on one path, which is aliasing, and the compiler must refuse it.

 This pins the protection rather than the defect. sb-append threads the record
 onward, so the checker treats it as consuming; were the signature ever changed
 so that it merely borrowed, the aliasing above would start compiling and the
 in-place append would corrupt both results with nothing to say so.

 The local-bound-from-a-call half of the same aliasing is caught too,
 since the minted-mutable walk; its pin is the sibling
 test stringbuilder-alias-local. Together the two hold both ways a
 builder can arrive.

 We say:

Section: Probe

  sba-two-owners : StringBuilder -> Text
  sba-two-owners (base) =
    let a = sb-append base "alpha"
    in let b = sb-append base "beta"
    in sb-to-text a & "|" & sb-to-text b

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni (sba-two-owners sb-new)
  end
